  "answer": "In humans, sex determination occurs through the combination of sex chromosomes present in the gametes (sperm and ovum) of the parents. Each human cell contains 23 pairs of chromosomes, of which 22 pairs are autosomes (non-sex chromosomes), and the remaining pair are sex chromosomes. Females have two X chromosomes (XX), while males have one X and one Y chromosome (XY). All the ova (eggs) produced by a woman contain only X chromosomes. The sperm produced by a man, however, can have either an X or a Y chromosome. If a sperm carrying an X chromosome fertilizes the ovum, the resulting zygote will have XX chromosomes and develop into a female. If a sperm carrying a Y chromosome fertilizes the ovum, the resulting zygote will have XY chromosomes and develop into a male. Thus, the sex of the baby is determined by the type of sperm that fertilizes the egg.",
