  "answer": "Mendeleev’s periodic table had several limitations. One major limitation was the presence of anomalous pairs of elements, where elements with higher atomic weights were placed before those with lower atomic weights. For example, tellurium (Te) with an atomic weight of 127.6 was placed before iodine (I) with an atomic weight of 126.9. This caused confusion in the arrangement. Additionally, dissimilar elements were sometimes placed together in the same group, which did not reflect their chemical and physical properties accurately. Another limitation was that the periodic table was based on atomic weights, which led to inaccuracies when atomic weights were not precisely measured. The modern periodic table addresses these limitations by arranging elements based on their atomic numbers instead of atomic weights. This change resolved the issue of anomalous pairs and ensured that elements with similar properties are grouped together. The modern periodic table, introduced by H.J. Moseley, is based on the atomic number, which is a more fundamental property of an element than atomic weight.",
  "selected_evidence_ids": ["E1", "E2", "E3"],
